.TH Tcl_Grapheme 3 9.2 Tcl "Tcl Library Procedures"
.so man.macros
.BS
'\" Note:  do not modify the .SH NAME line immediately below!
.SH NAME
Tcl_GraphemeCount, Tcl_GraphemeNext, Tcl_GraphemePrev, Tcl_GraphemeIndex, Tcl_GraphemeRange, Tcl_GraphemeReverse, Tcl_GraphemeUniCount, Tcl_GraphemeUniNext, Tcl_GraphemeUniPrev, Tcl_GraphemeUniIndex, Tcl_GraphemeUniRange, Tcl_GraphemeUniReverse \- routines for operating on extended grapheme clusters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fB#include <tcl.h>\fR
.sp
Tcl_Size
\fBTcl_GraphemeCount\fR(\fItutf8Ptr, numBytes\fR)
.sp
char *
\fBTcl_GraphemeNext\fR(\fItutf8Ptr, numBytes, grWidthPtr\fR)
.sp
char *
\fBTcl_GraphemePrev\fR(\fItutf8Ptr, numBytes, grWidthPtr\fR)
.sp
char *
\fBTcl_GraphemeIndex\fR(\fItutf8Ptr, numBytes, grIndex, grWidthPtr, grNumBytesPtr\fR)
.sp
char *
\fBTcl_GraphemeRange\fR(\fItutf8Ptr, numBytes, grFirst, grLast, endPtrPtr\fR)
.sp
Tcl_Size
\fBTcl_GraphemeReverse\fR(\fItutf8Ptr, numBytes, outPtr, outCapacity\fR)
.sp
Tcl_Size
\fBTcl_GraphemeUniCount\fR(\fIuniPtr, uniLen\fR)
.sp
Tcl_UniChar *
\fBTcl_GraphemeUniNext\fR(\fIuniPtr, uniLen, grWidthPtr\fR)
.sp
Tcl_UniChar *
\fBTcl_GraphemeUniPrev\fR(\fIuniPtr, uniLen, grWidthPtr\fR)
.sp
Tcl_UniChar *
\fBTcl_GraphemeUniIndex\fR(\fIuniPtr, uniLen, grIndex, grWidthPtr, grNumBytesPtr\fR)
.sp
Tcl_UniChar *
\fBTcl_GraphemeUniRange\fR(\fIuniPtr, uniLen, grFirst, grLast, uniEndPtrPtr\fR)
.sp
Tcl_Size
\fBTcl_GraphemeUniReverse\fR(\fIuniPtr, uniLen, uniOutPtr, outCapacity\fR)
.SH ARGUMENTS
.AS "const Tcl_UniChar" *grNumBytesPtr in/out
.AP "const char" *tutf8Ptr in
Pointer to a sequence of bytes in Tcl's modified UTF-8 encoding (TUTF-8).
Must not be NULL.
.AP Tcl_Size numBytes in
Length in bytes of the sequence pointed to by \fItutf8Ptr\fR. A negative
value indicates that the sequence is terminated by a nul byte.
.AP "const Tcl_UniChar" *uniPtr in
Pointer to a sequence of \fBTcl_UniChar\fR values. Must not be NULL.
.AP Tcl_Size uniLen in
Length in \fBTcl_UniChar\fR units of the sequence pointed to by
\fIuniPtr\fR. A negative value indicates that the sequence is terminated by
a nul \fBTcl_UniChar\fR.
.AP Tcl_Size *grWidthPtr out
If not NULL, the number of code points comprising the returned grapheme is
stored in the location pointed to.
.AP Tcl_Size *grNumBytesPtr out
If not NULL, the number of bytes comprising the returned grapheme is stored
in the location pointed to.
.AP Tcl_Size grIndex in
Index of a grapheme in the input sequence, counted in graphemes from 0.
.AP Tcl_Size grFirst in
Index of the first grapheme in a range of graphemes.
.AP Tcl_Size grLast in
Index of the last grapheme in a range of graphemes.
.AP char **endPtrPtr out
Location in which to store a pointer to the byte following the last grapheme
in a range.
.AP Tcl_UniChar **uniEndPtrPtr out
Location in which to store a pointer to the \fBTcl_UniChar\fR following the
last grapheme in a range.
.AP char *outPtr out
Output buffer in which \fBTcl_GraphemeReverse\fR stores a TUTF-8 sequence.
.AP Tcl_UniChar *uniOutPtr out
Output buffer in which \fBTcl_GraphemeUniReverse\fR stores a
\fBTcl_UniChar\fR sequence.
.AP Tcl_Size outCapacity in
Size of the output buffer in the units of the buffer: bytes for
\fBTcl_GraphemeReverse\fR and \fBTcl_UniChar\fR values for
\fBTcl_GraphemeUniReverse\fR.
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
These functions operate on \fIextended grapheme clusters\fR, referred to
simply as \fIgraphemes\fR in this manual page. A grapheme is what a user
perceives as a single character and may consist of several Unicode code
points, for example a base letter followed by a combining mark. See Unicode
Standard Annex #29 for the formal definition. Only operations that are
independent of locale are provided.
.PP
The functions form two analogous sets. The functions with names of the
form \fBTcl_Grapheme\fIXxx\fR operate on strings in Tcl's modified UTF-8
encoding (TUTF-8), and are passed a pointer and a length in bytes. The
functions with names of the form \fBTcl_GraphemeUni\fIXxx\fR operate on
sequences of \fBTcl_UniChar\fR values and are passed a pointer and a length
in \fBTcl_UniChar\fR units. The two sets behave identically except for the
units in which pointers, lengths and sizes are expressed.
.PP
Except where noted otherwise below:
.IP \(bu 3
\fItutf8Ptr\fR and \fIuniPtr\fR must not be NULL. This is not checked, and it
is the caller's responsibility to pass valid pointers.
.IP \(bu 3
\fIgrWidthPtr\fR and \fIgrNumBytesPtr\fR may be NULL, in which case the
corresponding value is not returned.
.IP \(bu 3
If \fInumBytes\fR or \fIuniLen\fR is negative, the input sequence is taken to
be nul terminated.
.PP
\fBTcl_GraphemeCount\fR and \fBTcl_GraphemeUniCount\fR return the number of
graphemes in a TUTF-8 or \fBTcl_UniChar\fR input sequence respectively.
.PP
\fBTcl_GraphemeNext\fR and \fBTcl_GraphemeUniNext\fR return a pointer to the
location following the leading grapheme of the input sequence. If the
sequence has zero length, the functions return NULL and, if \fIgrWidthPtr\fR
is not NULL, store 0 in the location it points to.
.PP
\fBTcl_GraphemePrev\fR and \fBTcl_GraphemeUniPrev\fR return a pointer to the
starting location of the grapheme preceding the end of the input sequence.
The length of the sequence, passed in \fInumBytes\fR or \fIuniLen\fR, must be
positive, otherwise the functions return NULL and store 0 in
\fIgrWidthPtr\fR if it is not NULL.
.PP
The length of the grapheme in code points is returned through
\fIgrWidthPtr\fR when it is not NULL. The caller can therefore step through a
string grapheme by grapheme in either direction.
.PP
\fBTcl_GraphemeIndex\fR and \fBTcl_GraphemeUniIndex\fR return a pointer to the
location of the grapheme at grapheme index \fIgrIndex\fR in the input
sequence. If \fIgrIndex\fR is out of bounds, the functions return NULL and
store 0 in the locations pointed to by \fIgrWidthPtr\fR and
\fIgrNumBytesPtr\fR, if they are not NULL.
.PP
\fBTcl_GraphemeRange\fR and \fBTcl_GraphemeUniRange\fR identify the range of
graphemes between the grapheme indices \fIgrFirst\fR and \fIgrLast\fR, both
inclusive. The return value is a pointer to the location of the first grapheme
in the range. The location pointed to by \fIendPtrPtr\fR and
\fIuniEndPtrPtr\fR, respectively, is set to the location following the last
grapheme in the range. A negative value of \fIgrFirst\fR is treated as 0. A
negative value of \fIgrLast\fR is treated as the index of the last grapheme.
.PP
\fBTcl_GraphemeReverse\fR and \fBTcl_GraphemeUniReverse\fR store the
graphemes of the input sequence in reverse order into the buffer pointed to
by \fIoutPtr\fR or \fIuniOutPtr\fR respectively. \fIoutCapacity\fR is the
size of that buffer in bytes or \fBTcl_UniChar\fR units respectively. The
functions return the number of graphemes stored in the output buffer, or \-1
if the buffer is too small.
.SS "INVALID ENCODINGS"
.PP
In the TUTF-8 functions, invalid encoded sequences are treated as grapheme
separators. The results of the functions are not guaranteed to be consistent
with one another when invalid sequences are present. For example, reversing a
sequence of invalid fragments may produce a valid encoded sequence. The code
point(s) returned as the grapheme for an invalid sequence should not be relied
upon, as they depend on how Tcl itself treats invalid encodings, which is not
consistent even within Tcl. The width of such a grapheme, however, is
consistent and may be used to continue iterating through the string.
.PP
Invalid sequences cannot occur in the \fBTcl_UniChar\fR based functions, as
each \fBTcl_UniChar\fR is a complete Unicode code point.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
Tcl_Utf(3), grapheme(n), string(n)
.SH KEYWORDS
extended grapheme cluster, grapheme, Unicode
